re devastating  for the creek nation  the creek war of 1813 and 14  which was the southern front of the war  of 1812.  we tend to forget that the war of 1812  was every bit as much a war against the  native americans as it was against the  british  in many in many uh cases the british and  the  native american tribes were allied  so there were actually three fronts in  the war of 1812 there was the niagara  front  there was the front in upper canada  centered around detroit where tecumseh  fought with the british  and then there was the creek war down  south  by the end of the war of 1812 which was  the last battle you recall was january  8th  1815 the battle of new orleans  the entire country  throughout the mississippi valley and  ohio valleys  and southeast with the one exception  of the seminoles who carried on until  the eighteen fifties  but the ohio valley the mississippi  valley  and the southeastern part of the country  indian resistance was crushed  moreover the british and french no  longer had a claim  on the united states  and when the spanish were driven from  florida five years later  the entire land from the eastern  seaboard  on the east to the end of the louisiana  purchase on the  on the west from the canadian border  to the mexican border and the gulf of  mexico  was u.s land many contemporaries  referred to the war of 1812 as the  second american revolution  i think a better way of putting it would  be it was the war that consolidated the  american revolution because this  territory was now  the united states the third story  involves an unspeakably  horrible and brutal murder by  two nephews of thomas jefferson  isham and lilburn lewis had moved to  western kentucky from virginia  and they were living in the southwestern  most  corner of kentucky as it was constituted  at that time  they murdered one of their slaves on the  very eve of the earthquake  december 15 1811.  two o'clock in the morning  on december 16th the first quake hit  the murder was brought to light by the  earthquakes  the fourth story is the story of the  steamboat new orleans  the very first steamboat on the ohio and  mississippi rivers  if you recall robert fulton's claremont  had successfully negotiated the trip  from new york city to albany just three  years earlier  and nicholas and lydia roosevelt the  great grand uncle and great grand aunt  of theodore roosevelt  set out to prove that the western rivers  were capable of steamboat travel  there was some skepticism about this and  for good reasons  unlike the hudson river where fulton's  claremont  made its historic voyage the ohio and  mississippi  especially the mississippi were rather  more wild  and untamed rivers the currents and the  hazards  were extremely great  and nobody believed  that a steamboat could make it  down much less up river  on the mississippi  lydia roosevelt was a remarkable woman  a year before the steamboat voyage they  had undertaken a flat boat voyage  to gauge the rivers  the voyage began when she was  several months pregnant and  by the time the flat boat reached new  orleans  she was eight months pregnant  when they returned to new york by  sailing ship she gave birth to her first  child a daughter when the new orleans  left pittsburgh in october 1811  lydia roosevelt was eight months  pregnant  and gave birth to her second child a son  on the steamboat  while it was docked in louisville  waiting for the falls  to rise so they could overcome that  hazard  the steamboat new orleans the maiden  voyage  of any steamboat on  the ohio and mississippi rivers  was caught in the earthquakes  the first earthquake hit just as the  boat was near cairo what's now  cairo  the steamboat survived that disaster  quite admirably unlike most of the other  boats on the river which were  swamped and tossed about like toys in a  bathtub  and proved conclusively that  steamboats were suited for  the western rivers  the fifth story of course is the story  of the new madrid 