merican  internment camp during  world war ii  and i turned my attention to a new  subject  and these earthquakes surfaced from the  back of my mental filing cabinet  i thought aha i'll look into that  it wasn't too long after i started  researching this subject  that i discovered i couldn't  possibly write this story as fiction  it was just simply too far-fetched  the confluence of forces and events the  coincidences  that came into play here  would have seemed like too much of a  stretch  to write as a novel  i didn't have to invent anything it was  all in the historical record  i just had to dig it out  so i decided to write a non-fiction book  two other things really appealed to me  about this story  i started to see the earthquakes as as a  symbol a metaphor if you will  for the turmoil and the upheaval on the  early frontier  that they came to represent for me  something much larger than  just seismological and geological events  they came to represent the chaos  that was occurring at that place and  time  in almost every aspect of frontier life  and i also saw i was struck  by how many parallels there are  in these stories to themes and issues  that are still with us  as americans  so i set out to write a proposal for  this book that's the way it works in the  non-fiction world you write  as opposed to fiction where you write  the novel  and your agent sends out the whole book  in non-fiction you write a proposal  and the better it is the more extensive  the more convincing the more compelling  the writing  the more likely you are to sell it and  the more money you're likely to get is  in advance  i spent about six months  on the proposal itself it was fairly  extensive  it was around 110 pages it was finished  in the  early summer of 2001  but the publishing industry shuts down  for the summer in new york  so my agent said  we'll send it out right after labor day  well right after labor day the world  trade center was attacked on september  11th  and for about three months publishers  weren't buying anything but books on  terrorism and islam  and political analyses and things like  that  so now we're approaching the holidays  and the publishing industry shuts down  for the holidays in new york and my  agent said well we'll send out after new  year  i was checking my account balance in my  checking account  he did send it out right after the first  of the year  and several houses were interested and  we decided to  sign with simon schuster we very much  liked the way they were approaching the  project  the book was two years in the writing  and another year in production and it  was released on march 10th  a little more than a month ago  as far as why there are earthquakes in  the mississippi valley  a study of the earth's crust reveals a  cyclical  process it's called  continental drift and i'm sure many of  you are familiar with it  over millions and millions of years the  continents drift together  and slam into one another and  form one or perhaps two super continents  huge bodies of land  over the course of millions of years  again  those can't supercontinents  break up into smaller continents  and then they come back together and  then they break up again  and nobody knows how many times this has  happened  the last breakup of the continents  produced the pres  predecessor of north america which  geologists refer to as laurentia  the shape of that continent was a little  different than the  continent we live on what is now the  gulf of mexico  extended up into what is now the  mississippi valley  so that the entire area  of louisiana much of southern alabama  a lot of east texas parts of arkansas  were all underwater  at some point another rift started  right at that at the apex of that  body of water the continent started to  break apart right there  as if the whole area  from what is now the mississippi valley  to the east coast  would have floated off and formed a new  continent  the earth's crust tore  magma molten lava came bubbling up from  below  spread out over the land  for some reason the rift did not  complete itself  but when the lava cooled  the crust of the earth sank under  much the same conditions that when you  take a cake out of the oven  under the wrong circumstances it can  fall  so now we have this huge failed rift  valley this sunken land  350 miles long 150 miles  50 to 100 miles wide  that exists as a scar  in the earth's crust  much much later when the great inland  sea  that covered in much of north america  began to drain  that was the path it took the path of  least resistance through this  huge rift valley  this became the mississippi river  the water brought down with it billions  and billions of  tons of soil and earth  and silt and filled in  those areas that are now east texas  louisiana  alabama  so we now have three miles of  silt this unconsolidated sediment  sitting on this bedrock  which still has this scar  which still present persists as a zone  of weakness  in the north american continent  unlike the area  areas where two tectonic plates come  together  california the west coast of the united  states all the way up to alaska  where tectonic plates are bumping and  grinding against each other constantly  and produce  a fairly high and frequent incidence of  large events in the  middle of the continent  the incidence and frequency of large  events tends to be  separated but when they happen they're  enormous  what causes these  under the atlantic ocean at the  mid-atlantic ridge  new crust is constantly being created  and as it's forced up to the surface  which is the floor of the ocean  it spreads out in both directions  as it spreads west it moves  the north american plate two or three  centimeters a year  well the movement of that creates  enormous  stress over time on  the fault zone that lies below the  mississippi valley  i don't i don't mean to alarm you all  here but it's still down there  paleo seismological studies indicate  that  there were a massive series  of quakes in  900 a.d and again in 1450 a.d  and then of course we know we have a  historical record about the 18  11 and 12 quakes so you could say that  every 350 to  500 years give or take  there is a major series of earthquakes  in the new madrid  seismic zone  the title of the book comes from  two particular incidents  on two different occasions during  the major  quake on  january on december  16th and the last of the three major  events on february 7th  a huge block of land was thrust up  i'm talking about a  piece of land thousands of square miles  this block of land went across the river  now it's like when you're in the bathtub  and you push the water towards the end  there's only one place when it can where  it can come that's back  to you so here you have the river  flowing  and this huge block of land blocking its  way  it flowed back upstream with the same  effect as a tsunami it's not a tsunami  because it's not an ocean phenomenon  and it's not created by the same  conditions that created tsunami but the  effect was the same  a 30-foot high wall of water ran  retrograde spreading out over the banks  flooding acres and acres and miles  on both sides of the mississippi river  and carrying both swamping many boats  carrying them upstream  even during the second incident carrying  them so far  up onto the land that when the waters  receded the boats were left high and dry  there's even a description of  fish being unable to swim  to keep up with the receding waters  and being left out on the land  the reason of course that the waters  receded so quickly is that this land  that was thrust up  is basically silt unconsolidated  sediment  and it wasn't going it wasn't long  before it was worn down  by the normal course of the river which  continued of course  so that's where the book takes its title  from  i would like to answer any questions you  may have  uh to the best of my ability there's a  microphone  set up on that side of the auditorium  and if you could make your way toward  that microphone  i would be very happy to field any of  your  inquiries  don't be shy now okay can you hear me i  sure can  okay i'm curious about uh the indian  tribe's ability to communicate  with each other to come to giving  speeches to various tribes  all the way from the northwest  territories down to the south how was he  able to  communicate effectively enough to you  know persuade them to join him  yes that's a good question and it's a  very simple answer  interpreters there were individuals  of one tribe or another who were  fluent in more than one language  he even had a translator  traveling with him so  the network among tribes was fairly  extensive  they all knew who each other were  and they traded and they fought and  so they had to have ways of  communicating i just wondered whether  there was a lingua  franca whether it was english french or  spanish  that they may have no it was it was  translators yeah right  you need to well the  no we need you to get to the mic because  uh  the the tv people really prefer that i  appreciate that  go ahead um yeah sure come on up  i apologize if you answered this earlier  but uh you said there were  major quakes in 900 and i believe 14  50. was that from uh indian accounts or  how did you get that no paleo  seismological studies  martisha tuttle and a a guy named eugene  schweig  from the u.s geological he's from the  u.s geological survey  in memphis tennessee i'm not sure where  martisha tuttle was based  but they went down to the region  uh around arkansas and dug up the land  that's what i mean when i talk about uh  the the uh the fellow who uh  who uh excavated in his backyard for me  with his backhoe you can see  the sand that was ejected right through  right through the silt you can see this  stream of sand  that's coming up that doesn't belong  there because it should be  way down below the surface and one more  question was the  did the earthquake occur during the  daylight hours or the nighttime hours  both the the first  the first major one occurred at 2 am  so you can imagine how terrifying that  must have been  and in fact many first-hand account  first-hand accounts say we thought it  was the end of the world  of course by daylight they realized it  was  an earthquake the second major event  happened during the daytime  and the third again happened in the  early morning hours  yeah please here in st louis the uh  story about the mississippi running  backwards has been part of the new  madrid story for a long time  in recent months after the tsunami  happened in southeast asia there have  been a series of presentations  by professors at washington university  from the earth sciences and  and geology departments and one of  some of these big researchers they  talked about you know why tsunamis  happen  and why earthquakes happen on plate  tectonics and all that stuff  and several people specifically asked  about the new madrid earthquake  and i specifically asked them the  question  did the mississippi river really run  backwards as we've always been told  and they said no that's a myth that is  impossible  that never happened and he explained all  the geological reasons why it never  happened so  i wanted to see what obviously it's the  title of your book so you must  believe that happened it's always been  the story that it happened but they're  now claiming  that that there's no real evidence that  that happens so what's your response to  that  well technically did the  mississippi run backwards no of course  not  but the effect of that huge block of  land  causing it to appear to the bystanders  if there's any doubt that that happened  just read the first-hand accounts i mean  they're in the book  but it's quite clear that people  who experienced those events believed  the river was running backwards and in  effect  it was was it really of course not  but if you were there i think the  technical 